# Line-ending policy for this repository.
#
# Three parties disagree about line endings unless this file settles it:
#   - this machine's git has `core.autocrlf=true`, which checks LF blobs out as CRLF;
#   - `web/.prettierrc` pins `endOfLine: "lf"`;
#   - the `prettier-web` hook in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` runs `prettier --write` on
#     every commit and rewrites any CRLF `web/**/*.{ts,tsx}` file back to LF.
# The net effect without this file is a working tree that is perpetually stat-dirty
# even though `git diff` stays clean. `.gitattributes` is the portable, per-repo fix:
# it overrides `core.autocrlf` on every machine and every clone, so nobody has to
# change a global git setting.

# Baseline: normalize every git-detected text file to LF in the repository AND check
# it out as LF in the working tree. This is the rule that overrides core.autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# LF-mandatory regardless of content detection. A carriage return on a shebang line
# is a silent bad-interpreter failure, and Makefile recipes are POSIX.
*.sh       text eol=lf
Makefile   text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Explicit source-language rules. Redundant with `text=auto`, but stated so the
# guarantee never depends on content sniffing.
*.go  text eol=lf
*.ts  text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf

# Binaries. `binary` is git's macro for `-text -diff`, which makes `text=auto`
# structurally unable to misfire on them. Only .woff2 and .ico are tracked today
# (under internal/webassets/build/client/ and web/public/); the rest are listed
# defensively so a future asset lands correctly on its first commit.
*.woff2 binary
*.ico   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
